  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper level low is located over Nova Scotia and it is\nexpected to slowly sit and spin over Atlantic Canada through\nmuch of the holiday weekend. As a result, an upper level trough\nwill be in place over the Northeast for the next few days, with\na light northerly flow in the low to mid levels.  \n\nDuring daytime heating today, a few showers may develop.\nAlthough moisture is limited, CAMs suggest that some showers may\nform over the Adirondacks and drift southward towards the Mohawk\nValley, Capital Region, Helderbergs and eastern Catskills. In\naddition, some showers may form over the high terrain of Vermont\nand push south across all of western New England. While a shower\nwill be possible, any precip looks fairly brief. Models aren't\nshowing much instability at all today, as an inversion around\n700 hpa will keep any clouds from getting too tall. CAMs show\njust 100-300 J/kg of SBCAPE, so most areas won't see any\nthunder, although a stray rumble cannot be totally ruled out.\nBased on this, there won't be any threat for severe storms and\nthe rainfall will be too light and brief to cause any hydro\nconcerns as well. Otherwise, there will be a partly to mostly \ncloudy sky and it will be comfortable with highs in the 70s and\ndewpoints mainly in the 50s.\n\nWith the upper level pattern remaining fairly similar, a stray\nshower can't be ruled out again tonight into Sunday morning\nacross eastern areas, but the lack of moisture and best forcing\nbeing off to the east will keep any precip fairly isolated. It\nwill remain fairly cool and comfortable through Sunday, with\ndaytime highs only in the 60s to mid 70s. There looks to be more\nsunshine on Sunday compared to Saturday, with the bulk of the\nclouds over the high terrain. \n\nBy Monday (Labor Day), the upper level low will be shifting far\nenough east to keep the area completely dry and surface high\npressure will be nosing into the area from the west. As a\nresult, there should be nearly fully sunny skies for Monday.\nWhile temperatures look a little warmer compared to Sunday, it\nwill still be pleasant and comfortable with highs in the 70s and\ndewpoints still in the 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe next frontal system to impact the region will be during the\nmiddle of next week. Based off the latest operational guidance,\nthe best large-scale forcing will be passing by to the north \nacross southern Canada, but the surface boundary will allow for \na broken line of showers. Models aren't showing much instability\nat this time, so even the threat for thunder looks fairly \nlimited at the moment. NBM has fairly high probabilities for \nprecipitation, especially for northern areas for late Wed into \nWed night, although guidance seems varied on the best timing of \nthe boundary at this time, so these POPs may adjust somewhat \nover the next few days. AL/ML guidance is showing only very low \nprobabilities for severe storms at this point, which makes sense\nwith the weak forcing and limited instability, which is typical\nfor this time of year. NBM only shows a 10-20% chance for 1\" or\nmore of rainfall, so rainfall amounts should be light enough to\navoid any hydro concerns as well. Overall, the pattern looks\nvery typical for early to mid September.",
